instruction:
This flowgraph is a filter test bench. It runs at a sample rate of 48 kHz with real (float) samples throughout. A Fast Noise Source generates Gaussian white noise with unit power (standard deviation 1), spread evenly from 0 to 24 kHz, and five FIR filters, whose taps come from the filter-taps variables, shape it. Each filter output is written as float samples to its own File Sink: the low-pass filter to lowpass.dat, the high-pass filter to highpass.dat, the band-pass filter to bandpass.dat, the band-reject filter to bandreject.dat and the root-raised-cosine (RRC) filter to rrc.dat.

I want to use the bench to split the spectrum around a channel that occupies 4 kHz to 7 kHz, with a 500 Hz guard band on each side of it (3.5 to 4 kHz and 7 to 7.5 kHz). Retune the flowgraph so that the low-pass filter keeps only what lies below the channel, the high-pass filter only what lies above it, the band-pass filter only the channel, and the band-reject filter everything except the channel. Each of these four filters must have unity gain in its passband and may roll off only inside the guard bands. Leave the noise source and the RRC filter exactly as they are: rrc.dat must keep receiving the same samples as now.

Measured on each file over 262144 samples after skipping its first 4096 samples, that means:
- lowpass.dat: at most 0.1 % of its power above 4 kHz, at least 24.0 % of its power between 2.5 and 3.5 kHz, and an RMS level of 0.39 (within 5 %);
- highpass.dat: at most 0.1 % of its power below 7 kHz, at least 5.3 % of its power between 7.5 and 8.5 kHz, and an RMS level of 0.83 (within 5 %);
- bandpass.dat: at most 0.1 % of its power outside 3.5 to 7.5 kHz, at least 25.7 % of its power in each of the slices 4 to 5 kHz and 6 to 7 kHz, and an RMS level of 0.38 (within 5 %);
- bandreject.dat: at most 0.1 % of its power between 4 and 7 kHz, at least 4.3 % of its power in each of the slices 2.5 to 3.5 kHz and 7.5 to 8.5 kHz, and an RMS level of 0.92 (within 5 %).
I worked out these numbers for unit-power noise and passbands that are perfectly flat with every band edge in the middle of its guard band: each 1 kHz slice next to a guard band must carry at least 90 % of the power it would carry then, and the RMS levels allow a little for the roll-off. Keep the 48 kHz sample rate and the five float File Sinks. Change parameter values only: do not add, remove or rewire any block, and keep every variable under its current name. The flowgraph should keep producing samples until it is stopped; do not limit the number of samples.
